{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with razor-sharp writing, gripping pacing, and a hauntingly atmospheric score. directed by david fincher, the movie transforms what could have been a dry corporate origin story into a tense, emotionally charged drama about ambition, betrayal, and isolation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but socially awkward prodigy whose genius is matched only by his emotional detachment. his portrayal is unnervingly precise — every twitch, pause, and monotone line feels calculated, mirroring the cold efficiency of the platform he builds.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a tour de force: rapid-fire dialogue crackles with wit and intellect, turning legal depositions into dramatic set pieces that feel as thrilling as any action sequence. the dual narrative structure, alternating between the 2003 founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuits from the winklevoss twins and christopher pruett, creates a compelling rhythm that keeps the viewer hooked. the courtroom scenes are especially brilliant — not because of courtroom drama clichés, but because they reveal character through language, revealing how truth becomes a weapon and memory a negotiable commodity.\n\njoseph gordon-levitt and andrew garfield bring nuance and humanity to their roles as the winklevoss twins and dustin moskovitz, respectively, with garfield’s portrayal of moskovitz as the loyal friend caught in the crossfire of zuckerberg’s ambition being particularly affecting. the film’s score by trent reznor and atticus ross is integral to its tone — a minimalist, electronic soundscape that evokes both the chill of the internet and the loneliness of its creator.\n\nvisually, fincher’s aesthetic is immaculate: the cold blues and grays of the harvard campus, the dim glow of computer screens, the sterile interiors of startup offices — all reinforce the emotional distance between people in a world increasingly mediated by technology. the film doesn’t glorify zuckerberg; it doesn’t villainize him either. instead, it presents him as a tragic figure whose greatest creation — a global social network — fails to connect him to anyone real.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about facebook; it’s a meditation on the cost of innovation, the fragility of friendship, and the loneliness of power. it captures the zeitgeist of the early 2000s tech boom with uncanny accuracy and foresight, predicting the social isolation and ethical dilemmas that would come to define the digital era. it’s a film that lingers — not because of spectacle, but because of its quiet, unsettling truth.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005540504935197532}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.571020781993866}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.294890433549881}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010425376240164042}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.005559459328651428}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00012957210128661245}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.352325201034546}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.009774088859558105}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.6411636471748352}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010501786891836673}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00025567124248482287}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.9047426581382751}, {"token": " razor", "logprob": -1.665907382965088}, {"token": "-sh", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "arp", "logprob": -2.3841